ionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beehive_Tombs,_Qubur_Juhhal_at_Al_Ayn,_Oman.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=18703581","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2012-03-14 21:23:34","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"Beehive Tombs, Qubur Juhhal at Al Ayn, Oman","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"Jebel Misht|Flickr images reviewed by File Upload Bot (Magnus Manske)|Files with derivative versions|Flickr images missing SDC creator|Bat, Al-Khutm and Al-Ayn archaeological sites|Nawamis in Al-Ayn, Oman|Photographs by Alfred Weidinger","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Camera: Fuji G617\nFilm: Kodak E100 VS\n</p>\nThe protohistoric site of Qubur Juhhal at Al Ayn lies near a palm grove in the interior of the Sultanate of Oman. Together with the neighbouring sites, it forms the most complete collection of settlements and necropolises from the 3rd millennium B.C. in the world. The 21 tombs, aligned on a rocky crest that stands out in the superb mountainous landscape of Jebel Misht to the north, are in a remarkable state of preservation. They have not been excavated and constitute an obviously interesting archaeological reserve.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2011-09-01 18:16","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/photos/a-weidinger/6103104219/\">Beehive Tombs, Qubur Juhhal at Al Ayn, Oman</a>\n</p>\n<ul><li>Uploaded by <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Tomer_T\" title=\"User:Tomer T\">Tomer T</a></li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/28523661@N07\">Alfred Weidinger</a